#import "@local/fgbooks:0.14.0": speech, stanza

== THE FIRST STAGE

As I walked through the wilderness of this world, I came upon a certain place where there was a den,#footnote[Bedford jail, in which the author was imprisoned for conscience’ sake] and lay down in that place to sleep; and as I slept, I dreamed a dream. I dreamed, and behold, I saw a man clothed with rags, standing in a certain place, with his face turned from his own house, a book in his hand, and a great burden upon his back. Isa 64:6; Luke 14:33; Ps 38:4. I looked and saw him open the book and read in it; and as he read, he wept and trembled; and, no longer able to hold himself in, he broke out with a pitiful cry, saying, “What shall I do?” Acts 2:37; 16:30; Hab 1:2, 3.

In this plight he went home and held himself in as long as he could, so that his wife and children would not see his distress; but he could not keep quiet long, because his trouble grew. So at last he opened his mind to his wife and children, and began to talk to them like this: “O my dear wife,” he said, “and you#super[+], my own children, I, your dear friend, am undone in myself because of a burden that lies heavy on me; what is more, I have been told for certain that this city of ours will be burned with fire from heaven; and in that terrible overthrow I myself, and you my wife, and you my sweet babes, will all miserably come to ruin, unless some way of escape can be found by which we may be delivered (and as yet I see none).” At this his family were deeply bewildered; not because they believed that what he had said to them was true, but because they thought some madness had got into his head; so, as night was coming on, and they hoped that sleep might settle his brains, they got him to bed as fast as they could. But the night was as troublesome to him as the day; so instead of sleeping, he spent it in sighs and tears. When morning came, they wanted to know how he was. He told them, “Worse and worse.” He also started talking to them again; but they began to be hardened. They also thought they could drive away his madness by treating him harshly and sullenly; sometimes they would mock him, sometimes they would scold him, and sometimes they would ignore him altogether. So he began to keep to his room, to pray for them and pity them, and also to grieve over his own misery; he would also walk alone in the fields, sometimes reading, and sometimes praying; and so he spent his time for some days.

Now I saw, one day when he was walking in the fields, that he was (as was his habit) reading in his book, and greatly distressed in his mind; and as he read, he burst out, as he had done before, crying, “What shall I do to be saved?” Acts 16:30, 31.

I saw also that he looked this way and that, as if he wanted to run; yet he stood still, because (as I could see) he could not tell which way to go. Then I looked, and saw a man named Evangelist coming to him, who asked, “Why are you crying?”

He answered, “Sir, I see by the book in my hand that I am condemned to die, and after that to come to judgment, Heb 9:27; and I find that I am not willing to do the first, Job 10:21, 22, nor able to do the second.” Ezek 22:14.

Then Evangelist said, “Why are you not willing to die, since this life is beset with so many evils?” The man answered, “Because I fear that this burden on my back will sink me lower than the grave, and I will fall into Tophet. Isa 30:33. And, sir, if I am not fit to go to prison, I am not fit to go to judgment, and from there to execution; and the thoughts of these things make me cry.”

Then Evangelist said, “If this is your condition, why are you standing still?” He answered, “Because I do not know where to go.” Then Evangelist gave him a parchment roll, and inside it was written, “Flee from the wrath to come.” Matt 3:7.

So the man read it, and looking at Evangelist very carefully, said, “Where must I flee?” Then Evangelist said (pointing with his finger over a very wide field), “Do you see that wicket-gate over there?” Matt 7:13, 14. The man said, “No.” Then the other said, “Do you see that shining light over there?” Ps 119:105; 2 Pet 1:19. He said, “I think I do.” Then Evangelist said, “Keep that light in your eye, and go straight up to it; so you will see the gate; and when you knock at it, you will be told what to do.” So I saw in my dream that the man began to run. Now he had not run far from his own door when his wife and children saw it and began to cry after him to come back; but the man put his fingers in his ears, and ran on crying, Life! life! eternal life! Luke 14:26. So he did not look behind him, Gen 19:17, but fled toward the middle of the plain.

The neighbors also came out to see him run, Jer 20:10; and as he ran, some mocked, others threatened, and some cried after him to come back; and among those who did, there were two who were determined to bring him back by force. The name of the one was Obstinate, and the name of the other Pliable. By now the man had got a good distance away from them; but they were determined to go after him all the same, which they did, and in a little while they overtook him. Then the man said, “Neighbors, why have you#super[+] come?” They said, “To persuade you to go back with us.” But he said, “That can by no means be. You#super[+] live,” he said, “in the city of Destruction, which is also the place where I was born: I see that it is so; and dying there, sooner or later, you will sink lower than the grave, into a place that burns with fire and brimstone. Be content, good neighbors, and come along with me.”

#speech[#smallcaps[Obstinate]: What! said Obstinate, and leave our friends and our comforts behind us?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Yes, said Christian (for that was his name), because all that you give up is not worth comparing with a little of what I am seeking to enjoy, 2 Cor 4:18; and if you will come along with me, and hold to it, you will fare as I will; for there, where I am going, is enough and to spare. Luke 15:17. Come away, and test my words.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Obstinate]: What are these things you are seeking, since you are leaving all the world to find them?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: I seek an inheritance incorruptible, undefiled, and that does not fade away, 1 Pet 1:4; and it is laid up in heaven, and safe there, Heb 11:16, to be given, at the appointed time, to those who diligently seek it. Read about it there in my book, if you like.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Obstinate]: Nonsense! said Obstinate. Away with your book; are you going back with us or not?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: No, not I, said the other, because I have put my hand to the plow. Luke 9:62.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Obstinate]: Come then, neighbor Pliable, let us turn back and go home without him: there is a whole company of these crazy-headed fools who, when they get hold of a fancy, are wiser in their own eyes than seven men who can give a reason.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Pliable]: Then Pliable said, Don’t mock him; if what the good Christian says is true, the things he looks for are better than ours: my heart is inclined to go with my neighbor.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Obstinate]: What! more fools still? Listen to me, and go back; who knows where such a brain-sick fellow will lead you? Go back, go back, and be wise.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: No, rather you come along with your neighbor Pliable; there are such things to be had as I spoke of, and many more glories besides. If you do not believe me, read here in this book; and for the truth of what it says, look, all of it is confirmed by the blood of Him who made it. Heb 9:17–21.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Pliable]: Well, neighbor Obstinate, said Pliable, I am beginning to make up my mind; I intend to go along with this good man, and to cast in my lot with him. But, my good companion, do you know the way to this place we long for?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: I have been directed by a man whose name is Evangelist to hurry on to a little gate that is ahead of us, where we will receive instructions about the way.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Pliable]: Come then, good neighbor, let us be going. Then they both went on together.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Obstinate]: And I will go back home, said Obstinate. I will be no companion to such misguided, fanciful fellows.]

Now I saw in my dream that, when Obstinate had gone back, Christian and Pliable went talking over the plain; and this is how they began their conversation.

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Come, neighbor Pliable, how are you? I am glad you were persuaded to come along with me. If even Obstinate himself had felt what I have felt of the powers and terrors of what is yet unseen, he would not have turned his back on us so lightly.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Pliable]: Come, neighbor Christian, since there are only the two of us here, tell me more now: what are the things where we are going, and how are they to be enjoyed?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: I can picture them better in my mind than speak of them with my tongue; but still, since you want to know, I will read about them in my book.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Pliable]: And do you think that the words of your book are certainly true?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Yes, certainly; for it was made by Him who cannot lie. Titus 1:2.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Pliable]: Well said; what are these things?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: There is an endless kingdom for us to live in, and everlasting life to be given us, so that we may live in that kingdom forever. Isa 65:17; John 10:27–29.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Pliable]: Well said; and what else?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: There are crowns of glory to be given us, and garments that will make us shine like the sun in the firmament of heaven. 2 Tim 4:8; Rev 22:5; Matt 13:43.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Pliable]: This is very pleasant; and what else?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: There will be no more crying or sorrow; for he who owns the place will wipe all tears from our eyes. Isa 25:8; Rev 7:16, 17; 21:4.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Pliable]: And what company will we have there?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: There we will be with seraphim and cherubim, Isa 6:2; 1 Thess 4:16, 17; Rev 5:11; creatures that will dazzle your eyes when you look at them. There you will also meet thousands and ten thousands who have gone before us to that place; none of them harmful, but loving and holy; every one walking in the sight of God, and standing in his presence, accepted forever. In a word, there we will see the elders with their golden crowns, Rev 4:4; there we will see the holy virgins with their golden harps, Rev 14:1–5; there we will see men who were cut in pieces by the world, burned in flames, eaten by beasts, drowned in the seas, for the love they had for the Lord of the place, John 12:25; all well, and clothed with immortality as with a garment. 2 Cor 5:2.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Pliable]: Just hearing this is enough to enrapture one’s heart. But are these things really to be enjoyed? How will we come to share in them?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: The Lord, the ruler of the country, has recorded that in this book, Isa 55:1, 2; John 6:37; 7:37; Rev 21:6; 22:17; and the substance of it is that, if we are truly willing to have it, he will give it to us freely.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Pliable]: Well, my good companion, I am glad to hear of these things: come on, let us quicken our pace.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: I cannot go as fast as I would like, because of this burden on my back.]

Now I saw in my dream that, just as they had finished this talk, they came near a very miry slough in the middle of the plain; and, not watching where they went, they both suddenly fell into the bog. The name of the slough was Despond. Here, then, they wallowed for a time, badly plastered with the mud; and Christian, because of the burden on his back, began to sink in the mire.

#speech[#smallcaps[Pliable]: Then Pliable said, Ah, neighbor Christian, where are you now?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Truly, said Christian, I do not know.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Pliable]: At this Pliable began to be offended, and angrily said to his companion, Is this the happiness you have been telling me about all this while? If we have such bad luck at our first setting out, what may we expect between here and our journey’s end? If I can only get out again with my life, you shall possess the fine country alone, for all I care. And with that he gave a desperate struggle or two, and got out of the mire on the side of the slough that was nearest to his own house: so away he went, and Christian saw him no more.]

So Christian was left to flounder in the Slough of Despond alone; but still he tried to struggle to the side of the slough that was farthest from his own house and nearest to the wicket-gate; and he got there, but could not get out, because of the burden on his back. But I saw in my dream that a man came to him whose name was Help, and asked him what he was doing there.

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Sir, said Christian, I was told to go this way by a man called Evangelist, who also directed me to that gate over there, so that I might escape the wrath to come. And as I was going there, I fell in here.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Help]: But why did you not look for the steps?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Fear followed me so hard that I fled the nearest way, and fell in.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Help]: Then, he said, Give me your hand. So he gave him his hand, and Help drew him out, Ps 40:2, and set him on solid ground, and told him to go on his way.]

Then I stepped up to the one who had pulled him out, and said, “Sir, since the way from the city of Destruction to that gate over there runs across this place, why is it that this patch is not mended, so that poor travelers might go there more safely?” And he said to me, “This miry slough is such a place as cannot be mended. It is the low place into which the scum and filth that come with conviction for sin continually run, and so it is called the Slough of Despond; for whenever the sinner is awakened to his lost condition, many fears and doubts and discouraging misgivings arise in his soul, and all of them get together and settle in this place: and this is why the ground is so bad.

“It is not the King’s wish that this place should remain so bad. Isa 35:3, 4. His laborers also, by the direction of his Majesty’s surveyors, have been at work on this patch of ground for more than these sixteen hundred years, to see if it might be mended. Yes, and to my knowledge,” he said, “at least twenty thousand cartloads have been swallowed up here, indeed millions of wholesome instructions, which have been brought at all seasons from every part of the King’s dominions (and those who know say they are the best materials for making good ground of the place), in the hope that it might be mended; but it is the Slough of Despond still, and so it will be when they have done all they can.

“True, there are, by the direction of the Lawgiver, certain good and solid steps placed right through the very middle of this slough; but at times when this place spews out a great deal of its filth, as it does before a change in the weather, these steps can hardly be seen; or if they are, men, through the dizziness of their heads, step off them, and then they are thoroughly mired, even though the steps are there. But the ground is good once they have got in at the gate.” 1 Sam 12:23.

Now I saw in my dream that by this time Pliable had got home to his house. So his neighbors came to visit him; and some of them called him a wise man for coming back, and some called him a fool for risking himself with Christian; others again mocked his cowardice, saying, “Surely, once you had begun to venture, I would not have been so base as to give up because of a few difficulties.” So Pliable sat cringing among them. But at last he grew more confident, and then they all changed their story, and began to ridicule poor Christian behind his back. And so much for Pliable.

Now as Christian was walking alone by himself, he caught sight of someone far off coming across the field to meet him; and as it happened, they met just as their paths crossed. The name of the gentleman who met him was Mr. Worldly Wiseman: he lived in the town of Carnal Policy, a very large town, and also close to the place Christian came from. So this man, meeting Christian, and having some inkling of who he was (for Christian’s setting out from the city of Destruction was much talked about, not only in the town where he lived, but it was also beginning to be the talk of the town in some other places)—Mr. Worldly Wiseman, then, having some guess who he was, from seeing his laborious going, and noticing his sighs and groans and the like, began to enter into some talk with Christian like this.

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Worldly Wiseman]: Well now, good fellow, where are you going in this burdened manner?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: A burdened manner indeed, as much as I think any poor creature ever had! And since you ask me, Where are you going? I tell you, sir, I am going to that wicket-gate ahead of me; for there, as I have been told, I will be put into a way to be rid of my heavy burden.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Worldly Wiseman]: Do you have a wife and children?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Yes; but I am so weighed down with this burden that I cannot take the pleasure in them that I used to: it is as if I had none. 1 Cor 7:29.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Worldly Wiseman]: Will you listen to me, if I give you some advice?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: If it is good, I will; for I am in need of good advice.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Worldly Wiseman]: Then I would advise you to get rid of your burden as fast as you can; for you will never be settled in your mind till then, nor can you enjoy the benefits of the blessings God has given you till then.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: That is just what I am seeking, to be rid of this heavy burden: but I cannot get it off by myself, nor is there any man in our country who can take it off my shoulders; that is why I am going this way, as I told you, so that I may be rid of my burden.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Worldly Wiseman]: Who told you to go this way to be rid of your burden?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: A man who seemed to me to be a very great and honorable person: his name, as I remember, is Evangelist.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Worldly Wiseman]: A curse on him for his advice! There is not a more dangerous and troublesome way in the world than the one he has directed you into; and you will find that out, if you let yourself be ruled by his advice. You have met with something already, I see; for I see the dirt of the Slough of Despond on you: but that slough is the beginning of the sorrows that come to those who go on in that way. Listen to me; I am older than you: in the way you are going you are likely to meet with weariness, pain, hunger, perils, nakedness, sword, lions, dragons, darkness, and, in a word, death, and who knows what else. These things are certainly true, having been confirmed by many witnesses. And should a man so carelessly throw himself away by listening to a stranger?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Why, sir, this burden on my back is more terrible to me than all these things you have mentioned: indeed, I think I do not care what I meet with in the way, if only I can also meet with deliverance from my burden.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Worldly Wiseman]: How did you come by your burden in the first place?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: By reading this book in my hand.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Worldly Wiseman]: I thought so; and it has happened to you as to other weak men, who, meddling with things too high for them, suddenly fall into frenzies like yours; and those frenzies not only unman men, as I see yours have done to you, but drive them into desperate ventures, to gain they do not know what.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: I know what I want to gain; it is relief from my heavy burden.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Worldly Wiseman]: But why will you seek relief this way, seeing that so many dangers come with it, especially since (if only you had the patience to listen to me) I could direct you to what you desire, without the dangers you will run yourself into this way? Yes, and the remedy is close at hand. Besides, I will add that instead of those dangers you will meet with much safety, friendship, and contentment.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Sir, please tell me this secret.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Worldly Wiseman]: Why, in that village over there (the village is named Morality) there lives a gentleman whose name is Legality, a very sensible man, and a man of very good name, who has the skill to help men get such burdens as yours off their shoulders; indeed, to my knowledge, he has done a great deal of good this way; yes, and besides, he has the skill to cure those who are somewhat crazed in their wits by their burdens. To him, as I said, you may go, and be helped at once. His house is not quite a mile from this place; and if he should not be at home himself, he has a fine young man for a son, whose name is Civility, who can do it (so to speak) as well as the old gentleman himself. There, I say, you may be relieved of your burden; and if you have no mind to go back to your former home (as indeed I would not wish you to), you may send for your wife and children to this village, where there are houses standing empty now, one of which you may have at a reasonable rent; food there is also cheap and good; and what will make your life all the happier is that you will certainly live there among honest neighbors, in good repute and good standing.]

Now Christian was somewhat at a loss; but he soon concluded, If what this gentleman has said is true, my wisest course is to take his advice; and with that he went on to say this.

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Sir, which way do I go to this honest man’s house?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Worldly Wiseman]: Do you see that high hill over there?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Yes, very well.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Worldly Wiseman]: You must go by that hill, and the first house you come to is his.]

So Christian turned out of his way to go to Mr. Legality’s house for help. But look, when he had now got close to the hill, it seemed so high, and the side of it next to the road hung over so far, that Christian was afraid to venture any further, for fear the hill would fall on his head; so he stood still there, and did not know what to do. His burden, too, now seemed heavier to him than it had while he was in the way. Flashes of fire also came, Exod 19:16, 18, out of the hill, which made Christian afraid that he would be burned: so here he sweated and shook with fear. Heb 12:21. And now he began to be sorry that he had taken Mr. Worldly Wiseman’s advice; and just then he saw Evangelist coming to meet him, and at the sight of him he began to blush for shame. So Evangelist drew nearer and nearer; and coming up to him, he looked at him with a stern and dreadful face, and began to reason with Christian like this.

#speech[#smallcaps[Evangelist]: What are you doing here, Christian? he said; at which words Christian did not know what to answer; so for the moment he stood speechless before him. Then Evangelist went on, Are you not the man I found crying outside the walls of the city of Destruction?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Yes, dear sir, I am the man.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Evangelist]: Did I not show you the way to the little wicket-gate?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Yes, dear sir, said Christian.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Evangelist]: How is it, then, that you have turned aside so quickly? For you are now out of the way.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: I met a gentleman as soon as I had got over the Slough of Despond, who persuaded me that in the village ahead of me I might find a man who could take off my burden.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Evangelist]: What sort of man was he?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: He looked like a gentleman, and talked a great deal to me, and at last got me to give in; so I came here; but when I saw this hill, and how it hangs over the way, I stopped short, for fear it would fall on my head.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Evangelist]: What did that gentleman say to you?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Why, he asked me where I was going; and I told him.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Evangelist]: And what did he say then?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: He asked me if I had a family; and I told him. But, I said, I am so weighed down with the burden on my back that I cannot take pleasure in them as I used to.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Evangelist]: And what did he say then?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: He told me to get rid of my burden quickly; and I told him it was relief that I was seeking. And, I said, that is why I am going to that gate over there, to receive further directions about how I may get to the place of deliverance. So he said that he would show me a better way, and a shorter one, not so beset with difficulties as the way, sir, that you set me on; and that way, he said, will lead you to the house of a gentleman who has the skill to take off these burdens. So I believed him, and turned out of that way into this one, hoping that I might soon be relieved of my burden. But when I came to this place, and saw how things are, I stopped, for fear (as I said) of danger: and now I do not know what to do.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Evangelist]: Then Evangelist said, Stand still a little, so that I may show you the words of God. So he stood trembling. Then Evangelist said, “See that you#super[+] do not refuse Him who speaks. For if they did not escape who refused Him who spoke on earth, much less will we escape if we turn away from Him who speaks from heaven.” Heb 12:25. He also said, “Now the just shall live by faith; but if anyone draws back, My soul shall have no pleasure in him.” Heb 10:38. And he applied them like this: You are the man who is running into this misery; you have begun to reject the counsel of the Most High, and to draw back your foot from the way of peace, even almost to the point of risking your perdition.]

Then Christian fell down at his feet as if dead, crying, Woe is me, for I am undone! At the sight of this Evangelist caught him by the right hand, saying, “Every sin and blasphemy will be forgiven men.” Matt 12:31. “Stop doubting and believe.” John 20:27. Then Christian revived a little again, and stood up trembling, as at first, before Evangelist.

Then Evangelist went on, saying, Listen more carefully to the things I am going to tell you. I will now show you who it was that deceived you, and also who it was he sent you to. The man who met you is one Worldly Wiseman, and he is rightly called so; partly because he has a taste only for the doctrine of this world, 1 John 4:5 (that is why he always goes to church in the town of Morality); and partly because he loves that doctrine best, for it best saves him from the cross, Gal 6:12. And because he is of this carnal disposition, he seeks to pervert my ways, though they are right. Now there are three things in this man’s advice that you must utterly abhor.

1\. His turning you out of the way.

2\. His efforts to make the cross hateful to you.

3\. And his setting your feet on the way that leads to the ministry of death.

First, You must abhor his turning you out of the way; yes, and your own consenting to it; because this is to reject the counsel of God for the sake of the counsel of a Worldly Wiseman. The Lord says, “Strive to enter in at the narrow gate,” Luke 13:24, the gate to which I am sending you; “for narrow is the gate that leads to life, and few there are who find it.” Matt 7:13, 14. This wicked man has turned you away from this little wicket-gate, and from the way to it, and so has brought you almost to destruction. Hate, therefore, his turning you out of the way, and abhor yourself for listening to him.

Secondly, You must abhor his efforts to make the cross hateful to you; for you are to value it above the treasures of Egypt. Heb 11:25, 26. Besides, the King of glory has told you that he who would save his life will lose it. And he who comes after him, and does not hate his father, and mother, and wife, and children, and brethren, and sisters, yes, and his own life also, cannot be his disciple. Mark 8:34; John 12:25; Matt 10:39; Luke 14:26. I say, therefore, when a man works to persuade you that the very thing without which, as the truth has said, you cannot have eternal life will be your death, you must abhor this doctrine.

Thirdly, You must hate his setting your feet on the way that leads to the ministry of death. And for this you must consider whom he sent you to, and also how unable that person was to deliver you from your burden.

He to whom you were sent for relief, Legality by name, is the son of the bond-woman who now is, and is in bondage with her children, Gal 4:21–27, and is, in a mystery, this Mount Sinai, which you have feared will fall on your head. Now if she and her children are in bondage, how can you expect to be made free by them? This Legality, therefore, is not able to set you free from your burden. No man has ever yet been rid of his burden by him; no, nor is any man ever likely to be: you#super[+] cannot be justified by the works of the law; for by the deeds of the law no man living can be rid of his burden. Therefore Mr. Worldly Wiseman is an alien, and Mr. Legality is a cheat; and as for his son Civility, for all his simpering looks, he is only a hypocrite, and cannot help you. Believe me, there is nothing in all this noise you have heard from these foolish men but a plot to cheat you of your salvation, by turning you from the way in which I had set you. After this, Evangelist called aloud to the heavens to confirm what he had said; and with that, words and fire came out of the mountain under which poor Christian stood, which made the hair of his flesh stand up. These were the words spoken: “All who rely on works of the law are under a curse. For it is written: ‘Cursed is everyone who does not continue to do everything written in the Book of the Law.’” Gal 3:10.

Now Christian expected nothing but death, and began to cry out pitifully, even cursing the time when he met Mr. Worldly Wiseman, and calling himself a thousand fools again and again for listening to his advice. He was also greatly ashamed to think that this gentleman’s arguments, coming only from the flesh, should have prevailed with him so far as to make him leave the right way. After this, he turned again to Evangelist, in words and sense as follows.

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Sir, what do you think? Is there any hope? May I now go back and go up to the wicket-gate? Will I not be abandoned for this, and sent back from there ashamed? I am sorry I have listened to this man’s advice; but may my sin be forgiven?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Evangelist]: Then Evangelist said to him, Your sin is very great, for by it you have committed two evils: you have forsaken the way that is good, to walk in forbidden paths. Yet the man at the gate will receive you, for he has good-will for men; only, he said, take care not to turn aside again, or you may “perish from the way, when His wrath is kindled but a little.” Ps 2:12.]
